@ls-ggg Follow-up: I added and ran a concrete manual E2E for the nested-mount use case discussed above.

The test restores an AppSnapshot while deliberately listing the writable child mount before the read-only parent mount. Each restore uses UUID-based volume names and a fresh container path, so it does not depend on a directory already existing in the template.

It verifies:

- the shared parent input is readable;
- writes to the read-only parent are rejected;
- writes to a read-only sibling agent directory are rejected;
- the nested agent-specific directory remains writable and the marker is observed on the host;
- the behavior remains stable across 10 independent restores.

Fixture and test commands:

    cubemastercli template create-from-image --image docker.io/library/busybox:latest --writable-layer-size 1Gi --cmd /bin/sleep --arg 86400 --cpu 100 --memory 128 --with-cube-ca=false --node <node-ip>
    cubemastercli template watch --job-id <job-id> --interval 2s
    cubemastercli template render --template-id <template-id> --json
    cd Cubelet && go test -c -o /tmp/integration.test ./integration
    CUBE_E2E_TEMPLATE_REQUEST="<cubelet_request JSON from template render>" RUNTIME=cube /tmp/integration.test -test.v -test.run "^TestAppSnapshotRestoreWithReadOnlyParentAndWritableNestedHostDir$" -endpoint 127.0.0.1:9999 -test.timeout 20m

Results on a clean KVM dev host with the PR Cubelet binary:

- nested restore E2E: PASS, 10/10 restores, 14.65s total;
- cubeboxcbri package tests: PASS;
- one-click smoke quickcheck: PASS.

The E2E currently remains manual because the repository does not have a Cubelet integration CI runner with KVM/AppSnapshot fixtures. The assertions are configuration-independent and accept any valid rendered snapshot request containing a long-running, busybox-compatible container.

